content.content:
In `parse_clause.c`, `transformLimitClause` handles LIMIT and allied clauses (OFFSET included). After transforming and coercing to `int8`, it calls the variable check:

```c
qual = coerce_to_specific_type(pstate, qual, INT8OID, constructName);

/* LIMIT can't refer to any variables of the current query */
checkExprIsVarFree(pstate, qual, constructName);
```

The helper's header comment explains its purpose: it checks that the expression "has no Vars of the current query level," noting this applies to expressions that "have to have a consistent value across all rows of the query, such as a LIMIT."

The actual check:

```c
if (contain_vars_of_level(n, 0))
{
	ereport(ERROR,
			(errcode(ERRCODE_INVALID_COLUMN_REFERENCE),
	/* translator: %s is name of a SQL construct, eg LIMIT */
			 errmsg("argument of %s must not contain variables",
					constructName),
			 parser_errposition(pstate,
								locate_var_of_level(n, 0))));
}
```

So a LIMIT/OFFSET argument referencing a column triggers error `ERRCODE_INVALID_COLUMN_REFERENCE` with the message "argument of %s must not contain variables". (Source: PostgreSQL `src/backend/parser/parse_clause.c`, PostgreSQL License.)

